# GitHub merged branch deletion

`delete-branch` adds merged-only branch cleanup to the GitHub App CLI. It previews by default and needs `--yes` for DELETE. It never changes App permissions.

## Contract

Supply repeated `--branch` names or `--branches-file`, not both. Ref-prefixed names are refused. Repository metadata supplies the default branch; branch metadata supplies protection. A missing ref is `alreadyAbsent`. A present ref requires verified protection metadata and a full commit SHA.

The branch lifecycle is serial: validate → protection → ref → compare → open-PR check → preview, or recheck → DELETE → readback. Only compare statuses `behind` and `identical` advance. An open pull request for `head={owner}:{branch}` refuses with `OpenPullRequest`, naming the PR numbers, including during previews. A moved ref stops before DELETE. Only HTTP 404 on readback proves deletion. A refusal advances to the next branch. A repeated rate limit stops API requests and records failures for the remaining inputs.

A request has two states: first attempt → one Retry-After cooldown → final attempt. Ordinary failures are not retried. The same installation Octokit owns both attempts. No extra client or dependency is introduced. Retry-After values longer than five minutes stop immediately with `RateLimited`; the error carries the reset time in `reset_at` and its message, and remaining branches are logged as failed without more API requests.

## Tag-preserved mode

Some branches can never pass the merge check: a parentless dist commit has unrelated history, and GitHub answers its compare with HTTP 404. `--preserved-by-tag <tag>` proves "nothing lost" another way, for exactly one `--branch`. It replaces only the compare step: read the tag ref, peel lightweight or annotated tags (at most five tag objects) to a commit, and require that commit to equal the branch SHA. The refusals are `TagMissing`, `TagMismatch`, `TagNotCommit` and `TagPeelTooDeep`. Confirmed writes re-peel the tag after the branch re-read; a missing or re-pointed tag refuses `TagMoved`. All tag refusals count as `refused`. Every other guard stays, and the mode makes no compare call. Records keep `compare_status: null` and add `preserved_by_tag`, `tag_object_sha` and `tag_peeled_sha`. Without the flag the output is byte-identical to before. The first user is a dist branch whose annotated tag peels to the same commit.

## Audit boundary

Open a private append-only audit sink before any branch work. Append and flush one JSONL record after each branch. A log failure stops further writes. Records contain the repository, branch, SHA, compare status, outcome, timestamp, CLI version and whether DELETE was attempted. They never contain tokens or raw SDK errors.

The default sink is `~/.local/state/shitrat/branch-deletes.jsonl`; `--log` overrides it. Parent directories must be real directories with mode 0700. Log files are regular files with mode 0600, opened without following a final symlink. Existing data is preserved.

## Limits

GitHub offers no compare-and-delete operation conditioned on the observed SHA. The immediate re-read narrows the race but cannot eliminate it. Keep branch writers paused during deletion. Readback failures do not trigger rollback. A DELETE attempt can succeed despite a later error, so inspect state before retrying.

GitHub can conceal permission failures as HTTP 404. The command verifies installation contents permissions and repository access before accepting missing refs; it cannot prove more than the API and permission metadata expose. A missing branch metadata response with a present ref is a loud failure, never proof that protection is off.

## Sources and verification

- Command and implementation: `packages/cli/src/commands/delete-branch.ts`.
- Registration: `packages/cli/src/cli.ts`.
- Fake-HTTP regression suite: `packages/cli/test/delete-branch.test.ts` and its preload fixture.
- Usage and operational limits: `packages/cli/README.md`, “Delete merged branches”.
- Gates: `bun run check`, `bun run test`, `bun run brain:check`.
- Safety plants: remove compare guard, accept ahead, skip moved guard, skip readback, delete during dry run, and drop the open-PR refusal. Tag mode adds: skip the peeled-SHA equality, skip the pre-DELETE tag re-check, skip protection in tag mode, call compare in tag mode, and accept a tag ending at a tree. Each mutation must fail its regression before restoring the guard.
